            0.101
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.106,
            0.75,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": "3. When you pedal a bicycle on a flat road, your muscles supply energy. In what forms does this muscular energy appear as you ride?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.167,
            0.369,
            0.19
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.3 Forms of Energy"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.192,
            0.661,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "As we have seen, energy is the capacity to do work. The examples that we have discussed till now correspond to the mechanical energy. But energy can exist in many other forms as shown in Fig. 7.10. It is possible to change energy from one form to another. For example, electrical energy is converted"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.33,
            0.182,
            0.366
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4 Grade 6 Curiosity Chapter 3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.276,
            0.661,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "into light energy in a bulb, and into thermal energy of the water in an electric water heater. The chemical energy in the food we eat powers our muscles and gets converted into mechanical energy. Similarly, a ringing bell converts mechanical energy into sound energy."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.38,
            0.661,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": "Among these, we will now look more closely at mechanical energy, since it is directly connected to the forces and motions you studied earlier."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.445,
            0.403,
            0.467
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.4 Mechanical Energy"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.469,
            0.663,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": "Mechanical energy is the energy that an object possesses due to its motion or position. Let us try to quantify mechanical energy by using the concept of work."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.53,
            0.334,
            0.55
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.4.1 Kinetic energy"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.552,
            0.661,
            0.606
          ],
          "content": "The energy possessed by an object due to its motion is called **kinetic energy**. All moving objects possess kinetic energy, such as a moving bicycle or a rolling ball."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.606,
            0.662,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "How much is the energy possessed by an object by virtue of its motion? It is common to define an object that does not move to have zero kinetic energy. Consider an object that starts from rest and acquires a certain velocity under the influence of a force F (Fig. 7.11). Then by the work-energy theorem, the work done by the force will equal to the energy gained by the object, which is the kinetic energy of the object in this case."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.729,
            0.316,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": "Initial velocity = 0 Initial Kinetic energy = 0"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.727,
            0.782,
            0.846
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.772,
            0.513,
            0.801
          ],
          "content": "Work done by force W = F×s"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.704,
            0.176,
            0.789,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.344,
                0.835,
                0.558,
                0.85
              ],
              "content": "Displacement along the force = s"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.861,
            0.175,
            0.912,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.676,
            0.25,
            0.818,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "**Mechanical energy** Energy due to motion or position of objects"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.705,
            0.298,
            0.788,
            0.359
          ],
